**Definition (Protected verifier)** · `def:home:protected-verifier` Protection::credential constructs one role's 64-byte lowercase hexadecimal password from 32 independently requested operating-system bytes, then requests its 16-byte salt separately, directly into protected storage. Credential retains two Home handles and no ordinary byte buffer. One page holds the password; the other holds a 135-byte verifier, its 16-byte salt and 263 scratch bytes, totaling 414 addressed bytes. The scratch consists of four 32-byte keys and a 135-byte comparison result. Each mapping is page-rounded, locked and protected before filling; a page too small for either region, an insufficient global budget or any acquisition failure refuses. Each role is a separate invocation; no role is derived from another. The generator establishes exactly two pages: the constructor’s demand guard refuses fewer but permits a larger establishment, so the generator must select the exact budget. An overlapping role is refused by the shared reservation guard while the first owns both pages. Pairing is recomputed from the same protected password and salt and checked across every verifier byte before return, with scratch erased on success or failure. This full-length source loop is not a compiler-level constant-time guarantee. Partial primitive writes cannot become an accepted credential: a derivation error refuses construction and drops the owned homes through ordinary best-effort destruction, not confirmed cleanup. A constructor refusal does not establish checked completion. Credential::complete attempts checked completion of both homes even when one refuses, and reports a fixed completion failure if either result is unconfirmed. The existing ownership refusal and cleanup limits remain; no accessor, generic callback, generic writer, descriptor handoff or serialization interface is public.

The private entropy-injection seam is used only with operating-system entropy in production and public fixture input in tests; the test-only comparison seam is owning-module access to the existing checked slice. It substitutes public fixture entropy and compares bytes inside the package without adding a production export. (`claim:crypto:protected-credentials-match-independent-vectors`) executes three distinct unsorted fixture inputs, compares every password and verifier byte reconstructed by ci/home_vectors.py, observes two page reservations and kernel locked bytes, cleared scratch and release of both homes. These Python hashlib/hmac/base64 expectations call neither workspace package nor its helpers. (`claim:crypto:protected-credentials-refuse-pairing-entropy-and-budget`) exercises corruption between construction and the real pairing check, independently in the prefix/work factor, salt, both separators and both key regions; first-request and second-request salt entropy failure after password filling; a one-page budget and overlap at exactly two pages. A test-only checked-completion entry counter distinguishes checked calls from Drop; either home’s injected release refusal still requires both checked attempts and both cleanup paths. Each retained failed mapping is confined to its isolated child. (`claim:crypto:protected-credential-errors-and-traces-contain-no-private-bytes`) observes a real wrong-pairing refusal through Debug, Display, source and primitive tracing, and checks the non-copying, non-displaying surface. The handle-layout assertion detects the tested retained-buffer mutation; it is not a general dynamic heap scanner. These controls do not prove entropy quality, erase compiler copies, admit primitive residue, or witness installation. The completion accessor still maps its internal access errors to ownership refusal: cache/release ordering makes a released access unreachable there and this cut gives completion callers no new reason to distinguish that inter