**Definition (Checked completion)** · `def:home:checked-completion` Home::complete consumes one home and returns a content-free ownership refusal before touching its mapping, or reports both unconfirmed cleanup flags after full-allocation erasure. The existing checked slice refuses a released home with a content-free mapping error and establishes ownership once; unlock and unmap use the same inventoried expressions. Successful unmap alone marks release and decrements the page-rounded reservation. The cached completion outcome prevents another attempt by Drop; a failed live mapping remains reserved until process exit, without returning another owner or a byte view. Ordinary Drop remains a best-effort fallback and retains the reservation when unmap fails. Abrupt death and unreadable ownership retain (`[ROOT-amend:crypto:secret-home]`)'s limits. Silently discarding a checked cleanup error would let a later generator report success without confirmed completion and is set aside.

The source-local entry/exit census for this operation is closed: the input is the moved Home handle and its already-owned mapping; checked_bytes checks public process metadata and lends the existing exclusive private slice; zeroize overwrites the complete allocation; unlock and unmap receive only the original pointer and length after that borrow ends; the output contains only ownership or cleanup flags. Debug, Display, Error::source and the instrumented return carry no protected byte; no new secret exit, derivation temporary, descriptor output or dependency is introduced. The finite controls allocate one page under the explicit 65,536-byte fixture budget, read mapped presence and locked bytes, and compare reservation deltas before and after each result. The finite controls also drop a home with an injected unmap failure and require retained mapping presence and reservation with erasure before the release attempt. A substituted foreign PID exercises completion through the real identity predicate, requiring ownership refusal, no mapping event and retained mapping, lock and reservation; it is not an executed fork. Synthetic ownership and cleanup refusals use test-only seams; a retained-mapping observer proves actual ownership independently inside Drop before checking fixture bytes, without manufacturing another owner. Deliberately retained mappings die with each isolated child. The duplicate-release seam observes a second call without executing a second unmap. This is conformance, robustness and test quality evidence for those finite cases, not the installation's syscall answers, a crash-erasure guarantee or admission of a generator.
